  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three ways of viewing a quantum-computing architecture, emphasizing how a standard gate model can be translated into a measurement-based photonic approach. In the upper left, a conventional circuit diagram shows several logical qubits evolving over time, with rectangular blocks representing gates acting on one or more qubits. In the upper right, the same computation is recast as a large two-dimensional grid of connected qubits arranged across logical qubits and time. The repeated lightning-bolt symbols suggest sequential measurements performed on this entangled resource state, illustrating the measurement-based model in which computation proceeds through local measurements rather than applying gates directly.\n\nThe lower panel then maps this idea onto optics. Single photons appear to be injected into multiple input modes of a linear-optical circuit, labeled as having \\(n\\) modes, and the outputs are sent to detectors. This visually conveys a photonic implementation where an interferometer or linear-optical network prepares and processes many modes, and the result is read out by measurement. Overall, the figure highlights a conceptual progression: gate-based quantum circuits, measurement-based computation on a lattice-like resource, and a practical linear-optical realization using photons, modes, and detectors.",
